-----BEGIN PGP SIGNED MESSAGE-----

Hello everybody,

For those of you who have waited (a long time) for an upgrade of the
Cyrix 5x86 kernel module hack, well, wait no longer.  I have decided
that I have procrastinated enough...


What is it?
- -----------
It is a kernel module to fiddle with the register settings of the
Cyrix 5x86 cpu.  It is meant for those who might think that their
motherboards do not fully utilize the CPU, or those who has nothing
else better to do.  The kernel module can mess with almost every
register setting of the CPU.


Credits
- -------
This program was derived from Christopher Lau's kernel module and
patch for enabling the L1 cache in some Cyrix 486 CPUs.  However,
very little, if any, is left of the original code.


What's new?
- -----------
Here's an excrept from the README file:

1) User friendly way to fiddle with the registers.  Just compile the
   module as is, and then use it as some kind of funky executable:

        modprobe cx5x86 btb_en=1 show_reg=1 ; modprobe -r cx5x86

   This way, you don't need to recompile the module everytime you
   want to experiment with another settings.  Cool, eh?

   Of course, if you prefer the old way of doing it, you can always
   edit the cxdefaults() function in cx5x86.c and then use

        modprobe cx5x86 use_def=1; modprobe -r cx5x86

2) Cleaned up the #include order, so let's hope we won't have
   any more of those module version not found errors ;-)

3) Improved Makefile so that it will automatically install the module
   in the right place if you do:

        make install

4) The L1 cache WB is disabled by default.  If you want it, edit the
   cx5x86.c file and uncomment the #define L1_WB line.


Example of usage
- ----------------
Here's an example to enable branch prediction, set load/store ordering
to weak, enable memory bypass, and enable fast FPU exception reporting:

modprobe cx5x86 btb_en=1 lsser=0 mem_byp=1 fp_fast=1 show_reg=1; \
modprobe -r cx5x86


