Following my post to c.o.l.a. about our port of selected NASA-developed
programs to Linux, I received a couple of queries about electronic
discussion forums for sites that are interested in using Linux in a
production or mission-critical capacity.  So far as I am aware, until now,
there hasn't been a newsgroup or mailing list with that focus.

I'm pleased to announce that COSMIC, NASA's Software Technology Transfer
Center, is now hosting the MCLINUX (for Mission Critical Linux) mailing
list.  The goal of the MCLINUX list is to facilitate discussion between
persons and organizations who are using Linux systems for essential,
production services.  Our definition of mission-critical is pretty
generous, so if you think your Linux system is mission-critical, it
probably is the type of system we had in mind when we started the list.

To subscribe to the MCLINUX list, send an electronic mail message to

	listserv@cosmic.uga.edu

with the line "sub mclinux <your name>" (without the quotes).  The subject 
line for this message should be blank.

If you have questions about the list, please send them to me directly
at kirby@cosmic.uga.edu.

We hope a lot of people will find this list useful.

- --steve	

