Announcing the availability of the Unibasic Compiler with versions for 
the Linux, MS-DOS, OS/2, OS9, and OS-9000 operating systems.

The UniBasic Compiler is well suited for both systems and applications 
programming and features the simplicity of BASIC, the structure of 
Pascal, and the utility and power of C. It allows those not proficient in 
C (or not inclined toward C) to develop system, utility, and application 
programs which are free-standing executables that will match the 
execution speed of any comparable C program.

The compiler features named variables and labels (as well as line numbers 
for compatabilty with older programs).

There are six data types including: BYTE, SHORT, LONG, BOOLEAN, REAL, and 
STRING. All of the data types (including USER-DEFINED) may be declared as 
simple variables, arrays (single and multi-dimensional), POINTERS, or 
VECTORS (based variables).

Complete compatablity with C functions is provided in both directions. So 
you can link to existing C libraries or actually write C library 
functions with UniBasic. FUNCTION POINTERS are also supported.

Control Structures include: IF/THEN/ELSE, WHILE/DO/ENDWHILE, 
REPEAT/UNTIL, LOOP/ENDLOOP/EXITIF, and FOR/NEXT.

UniBasic includes all of the statements and built-in functions found on 
most full featured BASICs as well as many others which extend the 
language.

Like most C compilers, UniBasic supports CONDITIONAL COMPILATION, MACROS, 
MANIFEST CONSTANTS, STRUCTURES, USE (include) FILES , EXTERNAL LINKAGE, 
INTERNAL FUNCTIONS, USER PROGRAM COMMAND LINE INTERFACE, and a full range 
of compile switches.

A fully functional FREE DEMO is available for the various operating 
systems mentioned which is limited only in the number of symbols allowed 
(and thus program size).

UniBasic was written in UniBasic...100%.

