Executor 2 allows serious Macintosh applications (and games too) to
run under Linux, DOS, Windows 3.x, Windows '95 and NEXTSTEP.

Executor 2 will be released on CD-ROM, and a beta CD-ROM has already
been pressed.  The CD-ROM contains Executor/DOS, Executor/Linux and
Executor/NEXTSTEP as well as a sample of Macintosh freeware, demoware
and shareware.  During the beta period, you can get the Executor 2
CD-ROM for $149.  That price allows you to run any of the three
versions of Executor contained on it, as long as you are only running
one at a time.  Beta CD-ROM licensees will also automatically be sent
the complete Executor 2 CD-ROM when it is ready (at least 4 weeks from
now, easily more).

More Executor information, including a demo version for Linux, can be
found at http://www.ardi.com/.  Additionally the Linux demo version
(as described in executor.lsm, included below) can be found in
sunsite's Incoming direcotory at tsx-11 as
ftp://tsx-11.mit.edu/pub/linux/packages/executor/executor-beta1-demo.tar.gz

The Linux version contains binaries for X-Windows as well as SVGAlib,
each of which is available as a.out or elf.

If you have a video card that SVGAlib knows how to linearly map, then
Executor is fast enough on a 66 MHz '486 or higher to play various
shoot-em-up style games, like Solarian II (Galaga-ish), Apeiron
(Centipede-like), Maelstrom (Astroids-inspired, although there's a
Linux port of this game already).  Marathon "limps", but my
understanding is the game is not yet playable.

Although we support other platforms, Executor is developed under
Linux, which makes Executor/Linux the likely flavor of Executor to
receive new features first (sound was supported in E/L before E/D).

In addition to our web site (http://www.ardi.com), the newsgroup
comp.emulators.mac.executor is another good source of information
about Executor.

