lirc (Linux Infra-red Remote Control) 0.2
Copyright (C) 1996 Ralph Metzler (rjkm@thp.uni-koeln.de)
(according to GNU General Public Licence 2.0 or later)

lirc is a Linux device driver that allows you to decode (and in a later
version send) infra-red signals of many (but not all) commonly used remote
controls.
The pre-release version 0.2 is available at:
http://www.thp.uni-koeln.de/~rjkm/lirc/lirc-0.2.tar.gz

It consists of a small loadable device driver and a daemon program.
The device driver (lirc.o) converts signals on the DCD line of the serial port
into a stream of integers containing the lengths of pulses and pauses in
microseconds. A diagram of a simple and cheap (<$10) receiver circuit is
provided.
The daemon (lircmd - lirc mouse daemon) tries to recognize codes of remote
controls in the pulse/pause stream and converts them into mouse signals
which are sent to the fifo /dev/lircm. It also makes the raw remote codes
available on a socket.

Also included is the client program "irpty".
It is used like this: "irpty cfg prog arg1 arg2 ..."
It connects to the server socket of lircmd and converts the received remote
codes into keystrokes for the program "prog" according to the config file
"cfg". A sample config file for the program "workbone" is provided. 
In the current configuration this allows you to control your cd player with
some Sony remote controls.

Don't expect much documentation. This is only version 0.2 and there is still
much to be done before lirc becomes easily configurable and usable. 
With this annoucement I only want to check out how the interest for such
a program package is. Any comments and suggestions are welcome.
The possible applications are obvious: Infra-red mouse, remote control for 
your TV tuner card or cd-rom, shutdown by remote (competition to jsrd :-) ) ...
For people with a little C/C++ knowlegde this should already be possible
with this pre-release. Otherwise, wait for version 1.0.

