"Hewlett-Packard Tells About Next Generation Unix"



Speaker          Eric Hamilton, Hewlett Packard Architech, Unix Technology

At the June meeting, Hewlett-Packard's Eric Hamilton, Architect, UNIX 
Technology will discuss HP's objectives and plans for realizing next 
generation UNIX, describe its underlying architecture, the technology 
initiatives for advancing the state of the art, and explain how HP's 
hardware and operating system strategies complement one another.

UNIX is a strategically important part of our lives and business.  Whether 
we realize it or not, UNIX enables us to do many of the things we may do in 
a day: use the phone, purchase fast food, use the Internet, book a hotel 
room, or airline flight.  In the future, however, computing, in general, 
will become even more pervasive and central to our lives -- enter the 
information utility.

Technically, UNIX has achieved some of it's major goals: standard API's and 
manageable environments.  It is now a stable and predictable envirorunent 
that can be trusted for empowered engineering and critical business 
applications.

For the ftiture, the focus now shifts to shaping UNIX to be central to and 
to enable the information utility.  To evolve into this role, the UNIX 
industry needs to grow its technical excellence, expand its application 
base, accelerate the time to market and lower the development costs for 
UNIX solutions.

Eric will tell us about HP's plans for helping shape that future.

