I have uploaded a cleanup release of the umsdos utilities.
There is a single new feature which allows some more control over 
recursion into subdirectory (option -R). Contributed by
Michael Nonweiler.
 
The source compiles cleanly with the following setup

	linux 2.0
	gcc 2.7.2
	libc 5.3.12

I have made the change (no-for-scope C++ feature) to accomodate older 
compilers too.
 
You will find

	umsdos_progs-0.9.src.tar.gz
	umsdos_progs-0.9.bin.tar.gz

at sunsite.unc.edu:/pub/Linux/systems/Filesystems/umsdos.

They are sitting in /pub/Linux/Incoming right now.

the umsdos utilities are needed to manage a Umsdos filesystems (umsdos 
allows you to use your DOS partition with the unix/linux semantic, being 
long name, permissions, owner, links, ...). The package includes

	umssync (the main utility) which is needed to synchronise
	umsdos with the msdos content. This utility is generally run
	at boot time, after messing with DOS

	umssetup allows one to change some mount time option of the underlying
	msdos fs, such as the default ownership and permission (for non 
	promoted directories). This is usually needed for the root partition.
	(Most of the effect can be obtain with "mount -o remount ... /")

	udosctl is a low level tools allowing one to operate on the 
	underlying DOS directory or the --linux-.--- independantly. Mostly
	used for testing

The package also include a man page for umssync.





