-----BEGIN PGP SIGNED MESSAGE-----

I've released a kernel patch to provide an interface which allows 
device drivers and modules to allocate very large regions of pinned
physical memory, e.g., for large DMA buffers. Such regions are required
for drivers which require contiguous physical memory areas larger than 
that provided by kmalloc() or other means. Examples include the Matrox 
Meteor framegrabber and drivers for various network interfaces.

The patch works by allowing the kernel to reserve a large portion of 
physical memory at boot-time for this purpose, portions of which can be
allocated and freed by drivers. If and when the kernel VM system allows
large blocks to be dynamically allocated, the driver interface for
'bigphysarea' will remain the same.

I realize that this is an imperfect solution to the problem of large
DMA regions; however, it does provide a generic interface the back-end
to which can be fixed at some later date. 

Pick it up from ftp.cs.cornell.edu:/pub/mdw/bigphysarea-1.3.71.tar.gz.
I've tested on 1.3.71 kernels but it should work with earlier and later
versions. 

Happy hacking,
mdw

