-----BEGIN PGP SIGNED MESSAGE-----

I am pleased to announce a voice communication application that I developed
for the Linux OS. This application which is called Ethernet Phone, offers
real time point-to-point voice service providing virtual duplex voice
communication over the Internet. I have tested the application on PCs
running Linux around the campus and it worked pretty well.

I also wrote a detail report on this application which may be useful for
those of you who like to hack the source code. Please inform me before you
make any enhancements to the software.

Both the software and the report can be obtained from my work related web
page. The URL is :

		http://www.wpi.edu/~murti/work.html

If you dont have a WWW access, you can ftp the software from:

		ee.wpi.edu from the directory /pub/ephone.

Below is the README file for this application.

- -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
		  ETHERNET PHONE v1.0 for the Linux OS.

			  By: Murtaza Amiji
	   Department of Electrical & Computer Engineering,
		   Worcester Polytechnic Institute
			    Worcester, MA.
		        E-mail: murti@ee.wpi.edu
		Personal Homepage: http://www.wpi.edu/~murti
		     ECE Homepage: http://ece.wpi.edu
	

			    INTRODUCTION

This software allows real time point-to-point voice communication over
the Internet.  The software is specifically developed for the ELF distribution
of the Linux OS.  

The application includes executable interface which is made 
available for ELF binary formats. 

The followings are minimum requirements needed to run this application under 
Linux:

1) Minimum of 32 Kbits/sec connection to the Internet.  

2) An ELF distribution of Linux.

3) Sound card (half duplex should do it), Microphone, & headphones/speakers.

		      THE E-PHONE DISTRIBUTION

The contents of this package are as follows:
/README		--	This file
/adpcm.c		--	Code for the IMA ADPCM coder/decoder
/adpcm.h		--	The include file for the IMA ADPCM 
				coder/decoder
/ephone		--	The GUI for E-Phone. The is the file you need 
				to execute to launch E-Phone.
/Makefile		--	Makefile to build the software.
/Sound-HOWTO		--	The Sound-HOWTO document.
/copyright		--	Copyright notice for this software.
/copyright.adpcm 	--	Copyright notice for the ADPCM coder/decoder.
/copyright.xmix	--	Copyright notice for the Xmix Linux Sound mixer
/phone.au		--	Telephone ring file used by the daemon.
/ephone-FAQ		--	The E-Phone FAQ.
/ephone_client.c   	-- 	Code for the E-Phone client.
/ephone_server.c   	--	Code for the E-Phone server.
/ephoned.c  		--	Code for the E-Phone daemon.
/interface/    	-- 	The tclX scripts for the E-Phone GUI. 
/xmix.static   	--	The Xmix sound mixer for the Linux OS.

The distribution include two program that were obtained from the public domain.
These are the ADPCM coder/decoder and the Xmix static sound mixer.

The IMA ADPCM coder/decoder is written by Stichting Mathematisch Centrum,
Amsterdam, The Netherlands. Please read the copyright.adpcm for more 
information on the copyright of this compression.

Xmix sound mixer is for X Windows. This is an adaption of Steve Haehnichen's 
<shaehnic@ucsd.edu> Sound Blaster control program "xmix" to the Linux's sound
driver v2.0. The author of the Xmix Xaw program is Hal Brand (brand@netcom.com 
or brand1@llnl.gov).

		      INSTALLATION PROCEDURES

Unpack the distribution by executing the following command:
	
"gunzip < ephone.tar.gz |tar -xvf -" 

cd to the ephone directory and type "make" to build the ephone_client, 
ephone_server, and the ephoned (E-Phone Daemon). 

			  HOW TO USE E-PHONE

VERY IMPORTANT: Always Make sure that the daemon is running on the
background before you launch E-Phone. To run the daemon. launch it by
typing "ephoned &" from X Windows. You can add a message in your
login file which reminds you to launch the daemon when you start X
windows.

Start E-Phone by simply typing "ephone &" from an xterm or rxvt. Refer to the
help menu on the interface window for additional help on how to use E-Phone. 

Note: 	You can also run this application without having to run X Windows.
	In both cases, you will still need to run the daemon in the background.
	To connect to an external E-Phone server, simply type 
	"ephone_client <hostname>" where <hostname> is the name of the host 
	you want to call. Make sure you have your sound mixer set to the right
	volume, recording level, and dsp level.

If you have any questions or comments, please feel free to write me an email at
murti@ece.wpi.edu. 

