-----BEGIN PGP SIGNED MESSAGE-----

I've written a small program which generates PostScript from ordinary
text.  If this sounds like the reinvention of the wheel for you, then
you might be right (I for myself already know `a2ps', `i2ps', `a2gs',
`a2x', `atp', `gslp', and `nenscript', but please read on).

What makes `textps' an extraordinary tool is simple.

* You can print on non-standard paper formats without recompiling the
  program.  Every tried printing on self-sticking address labels?

* Optionally adjust the output relative to the print engine's origin.
  A left margin, top margin, and binding offset can be specified too.

* Variable font size, top skip, and baseline skip.

* Does no excessive formatting itself.  On the contrary, `textps' uses
  the standard UN*X text utilities for that.  For example,

      textps --filter='fold -sw 80'
      textps --filter=expand --filter='cut -b 80'
      textps --filter="tr -d '\f'" --filter='pr -fl 56'

  are all quite useful examples.  As you can see, `textps' is fully
  compatible with KISS and YAFIYGI.

* Supports the ISO 8859-1 character encoding.  What?  You want to
  convert files written on a Mac.  Say

      textps --filter='recode mac:latin1'

* Obeys `nroff' style backspace conventions for bold and italic font
  attributes.  To print, for example, the manual page of `ls', say

      $ man -P'textps --nroff' ls | lpr

  `textps' uses a real italic typeface and it is even able to print
  bold-italic, isn't that nice?

* `textps' is distributed under the terms of the GNU General Public
  License so that the entire GNU community will benefit.

* As an extra bonus, the package includes `tops', a magicfilter(1)
  script which converts DVI, troff, ditroff, PBM, PGM, PPM, GIF, JPEG,
  TIFF, and ordinary text files to PostScript.  `tops' can be used as
  an input filter for lpr(1), or faxspool(1).


You can get `textps' from

    ftp://ftp.UL.BaWue.DE/pub/purple/misc/textps-1.0.tar.gz

or via my home page at

    http://www.UL.BaWue.DE/~rs/ftp.html#textps

- -- 
Ralph

